+// WAITING FOR XENSTORE MUST NOT FREEZE THE GUEST.
+//
+// StoreSubmitRequest below waits for its request to complete while holding Context->Lock at
+// DISPATCH_LEVEL, and XENBUS_EVTCHN(Wait) is a pure _mm_pause() busy-spin (evtchn.c). So the
+// original loop burned a whole core with the store lock held for up to XENBUS_STORE_POLL_PERIOD
+// seconds PER ITERATION - and, because a timeout only logged and iterated again, forever if the
+// request never completed. About ten xenstore entry points contend on that lock, so one stuck
+// request takes down every PV path in the guest: measured as qrexec, the PV console and even the
+// Windows event log all going silent together while vCPUs spun.
+//
+// Two things are wrong there and both are fixed: the unbounded retry, and the fact that waiting
+// costs a spinning core at all. A request is completed by whoever polls next - StoreDpc runs
+// __StorePoll independently - so this thread does NOT have to hold the lock, or the CPU, to make
+// progress. It now spins only long enough for the common fast case and otherwise SLEEPS with the
+// lock dropped, leaving the guest fully responsive.
+//
+// The sleep needs PASSIVE_LEVEL. A caller already at DISPATCH_LEVEL cannot sleep, so that path
+// keeps spinning - but under a tight deadline, which is still shorter than the single timeout
+// the original code allowed itself.
+#define XENBUS_STORE_SPIN_MS        1   // spin this long per poll, then stop burning the core
+#define XENBUS_STORE_SLEEP_MS       5   // sleep this long between polls (lock dropped)
+#define XENBUS_STORE_TIMEOUT_S      30  // overall deadline when we can sleep: no freeze, just waiting
+#define XENBUS_STORE_DPC_TIMEOUT_MS 1000 // overall deadline when we cannot sleep: bounded spin
+
